but B3 unshown count=1, maybe unlikely.  \n     - W8, W9 \u2014 need W7 for chow, W7 unshown=3 possible.  \n   - Potential pungs:  \n     - W4: we have 2 W4s, need third W4 (unshown=2 left), possible.  \n     - B2: we have 2 B2s, need third B2 (unshown=3 left), possible.  \n     - Any honors: F3 unshown=2, J1 unshown=4, J2 unshown=1.  \n   - Loners: T5, J1, F3, J2, etc.  \n\n3. **Danger assessment:** discarding safe tiles?  \n   - Based on history:  \n     Previous player discarded B5, B7, B1, T7, etc., but also melded a kong of T4.  \n     Across player melded F2 pung.  \n     Next player melded T6 pung.  \n   - Tiles already discarded by others might be safer to discard now.  \n   - **F3**: Has been discarded by across player earlier (Play F3) and by previous player (Play F3). Seems relatively safe.  \n   - **J1, J2**: J2 was discarded several times by opponents (next player Play J2, previous player Play J2 twice), so likely safe. J1 not yet discarded much (only unshown J1=4), maybe slightly more risky.  \n   - **B6, B7, B8**: The B6-B7-B8 chow is valuable; breaking it reduces hand shape significantly.  \n   - **W9**: Terminal, maybe dangerous but W9 unshown=4 (still many left).  \n\n4. **Keep the B6-B7-B8 chow** for sure. This is a natural meld, so discard from other groups.  \n\n**Candidate discards among valid actions:**  \n- **B2**: We have 2 B2, could form pung possibly, but B2 unshown=3, opponent might need. B2 discarded earlier by next player (Play B2), so maybe safe.  \n- **W8**: only 1 W8 in hand, no strong pairing (W7 unshown=3 possible chow), but W8 unshown=3.  \n- **W9**: terminal, but we may keep if aiming for outside hand, but we have only 1 W9, and it\u2019s far from other W tiles except W8.  \n- **B7**: part of the chow B6-B7-B8, so **do not discard**.  \n- **J2**: only 1 in hand, unshown=1 left, opponent less likely to have pung need, relatively safe.  \n- **T5**: middle tile, unshown=3 left, could be useful for chow with T4/T6 but T4 all gone (T4 unshown=0 due to previous player's kong), T6 unshown=0 (next player\u2019s pung). So T5 is dead for chow. Safe discard.  \n- **J1**: unshown=4 left, but if discarded, could be useful for Dragon Pung (J1 pung worth 2 points), but we only have 1 J1. Low chance.  \n- **F3**: we have 1, unshown=2 left, West Wind (F3) is our seat wind, so if we get pung of F3, we get Seat Wind 2 points. But only 1 in hand currently. Might be worth keeping for potential wind pung? Possibly.  \n- **B8**: part of chow, so no.  \n- **B6**: part of chow, so no.  \n- **W4**: we have 2, possible pung, keep.  \n- **B1**: terminal, only 1 in hand, could pair with something, but B1 unshown=2 left, not too dangerous.  \n\n**Best discards** that don\u2019t break chow and are safe-ish: **T5** (dead tile), **J2** (safe), **F3** (maybe keep for seat wind), **W9** (terminal, but maybe keep if going for Outside Hand).  \n\nGiven hand is still early (no melds yet), **tile efficiency** says keep flexible shapes.  \nBreaking the B6-B7-B8 chow is bad, so keep B6,B7,B8.  \nWe have B2,B2 possible pung; W4,W4 possible pung; J1 maybe get more; F3 maybe get more.  \n\nWe can discard **T5** because:  \n- T5 can\u2019t form chow (T4 and T6 are exhausted),  \n- It\u2019s not paired,  \n- It\u2019s a simple suit but no value for special pattern here.  \n\n**Evaluation**:  \nT5 discard is safe (no T4/T6 available for others to chow with it, and T5 unshown=3, so low pung risk).  \nKeeps all other potential melds intact.  \n\n**Final choice**: Play T5.  \n\n---\n\n**Evaluation**: T5 is inefficient and safe, other discards either break useful chow or discard potential wind/dragon tiles that could yield points.  \n\n**Reason**: T5 is dead for chow due to T4 and T6 exhausted; keeping it blocks no useful meld; discarding it preserves B6-B7-B8 chow and potential pungs in W4 or honors.  \n\n**Answer**: Play T5"}
